		<section label="States" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing open="false">
			<html>
				<ui-text paragraph>A <dfn class="spell-term" tabindex="0">state</><ui-popup heading="state" label="A CSS custom state the element sets on itself as it changes:  epic-aside:state(open)."></> is set by the element on itself; none is ever written in the doc.</>
			</>
			<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
				<row header>
					<cell>State</>
					<cell>When</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>open</>
					</>
					<cell>Unfolded: the reader opened it, or it started so.</>
				</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="5. How it looks">
		<icon slot="icon" name="palette"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>Its heading: always in full, as many lines as it needs, folded or open. The chevron and the slot's buttons sit on its first line, so nothing moves as it folds (2026-10-19).</>
			<item>The brand's code well, as the docs' code blocks (<text code>ui-accordion.spell-code</>): a frame with a hairline, its heading small, mono and muted, the code on a card inside.</>
			<item>Coloured by its <text code>language</> (else guessed), with a copy button: Spell UI's <text code>&lt;ui-code&gt;</>.</>
			<item>A long listing scrolls inside itself (at most 70% of the window's height); a wide one scrolls sideways. Never the page.</>
			<item>Folded to start with, unless <text code>open</>. A click on the heading, or find-in-page, unfolds it.</>
			<item>No state colour. It shares its shape with <text code>&lt;epic-aside&gt;</> (<text code>EpicPanel</>).</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="6. Written by">
		<icon slot="icon" name="terminal"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>Claude writes it inside the HTML it passes to <text code>epic</> (<text code>add ... --details</>, <text code>decide --details</>, <text code>details --file</>, <text code>updated</>): no command makes one by itself. Code in an epic page is ALWAYS one of these (<link title="epic/templates/epics/plan-doc.md" target="src-spell-epic-templates-epics-plan-doc-md" link="/epic/templates/epics/plan-doc.md"><text code>plan-doc.md</></>, "Prose").</>
			<item>The epic tool turns the OLD hand-shaped markup into this element, so a doc never holds the old shape. <list bulleted><item>It does this in any HTML a command takes: <text code>add --details</>, <text code>decide --details</>, <text code>details --file</>, <text code>updated</>.</> <item>The code that does it: <text code>IncomingHtml</> and <text code>ProseRewrite</>.</> <item>Prose edited by hand in a part file isn't passed through it.</> <item>The old shape: <text code>ui-accordion.spell-code</> (and its four wrappers).</></></>
			<item>The converter did the same to every converted doc, keeping <text code>title</>, <text code>language</> and <text code>open</> (<text code>ProseUpgrader</>).</>
			<item>On a details page it goes as plain prose: its title over its <text code>&lt;pre&gt;</> (<text code>PlanItem.asProse()</>).</>
		</>
	</>
	<html>
		<footer class="site-page-footer">
